                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Put ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "t=\\lvert x-1\\rvert\\ge0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", factor the quadratic in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "t",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then solve the two absolute-value equations.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Put $t=\\lvert x-1\\rvert\\ge0$, so $(x-1)^2=t^2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}t^2-7t+12&=0\\\\(t-3)(t-4)&=0\\\\t&=3\\text{ or }4\\end{align*}$$\n\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero. Both values of $t$ are nonnegative.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lvert x-1\\rvert&=3\\text{ or }4\\\\x-1&=\\pm3\\text{ or }\\pm4\\\\x&=1\\pm3\\text{ or }1\\pm4\\\\x&\\in\\{-3,-2,4,5\\}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p126-q62-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\lvert x^2-9\\rvert=\\lvert x+3\\rvert$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p126-q62-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x\\in\\{-3,2,4\\}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p126-q62-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Factor ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x^2-9",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\lvert uv\\rvert=\\lvert u\\rvert\\lvert v\\rvert",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", and retain the zero-factor solution.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Use $x^2-9=(x-3)(x+3)$ and $\\lvert uv\\rvert=\\lvert u\\rvert\\lvert v\\rvert$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lvert(x-3)(x+3)\\rvert&=\\lvert x+3\\rvert\\\\\\lvert x-3\\rvert\\lvert x+3\\rvert&=\\lvert x+3\\rvert\\\\\\lvert x+3\\rvert(\\lvert x-3\\rvert-1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, including the possibility $\\lvert x+3\\rvert=0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lvert x+3\\rvert&=0\\text{ or }\\lvert x-3\\rvert=1\\\\x+3&=0\\text{ or }x-3=\\pm1\\\\x&=-3\\text{ or }x=3\\pm1\\\\x&\\in\\{-3,2,4\\}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
